  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/F5BE37">Example <span data-tag="F5BE37">8.2.9.29</span>.</a> Let ${\mathbf A}$ be a simplicial model category, so that we have a left Quillen bifunctor </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \otimes : {\mathbf A}\times \sSet \rightarrow {\mathbf A}, \]
</div>
  <p> and consider the coend functor </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \int _{ \cDelta ^{op} } \Fun ( \cDelta ^{op}, {\mathbf A}) \times \Fun ( \cDelta , \sSet ) \rightarrow {\mathbf A}. \]
</div>
  <p> Let $\Delta ^{\bullet } \in \Fun ( \cDelta , \sSet )$ denote the standard simplex (that is, the functor $[n] \mapsto \Delta ^ n$), and let ${\bf 1}$ denote the final object of $\Fun (\cDelta , \sSet )$ (that is, the constant functor given by $[n] \mapsto \Delta ^0$). The unique map $\Delta ^{\bullet } \rightarrow {\bf 1}$ is a weak equivalence, and $\Delta ^{\bullet }$ is Reedy cofibrant: we may therefore regard $\Delta ^{\bullet }$ as a cofibrant replacement for the constant functor ${\bf 1}$. </p>
  <p>The functor $X_{\bullet } \mapsto \int _{ \cDelta ^{op}}(X_{\bullet }, {\bf 1})$ can be identified with the colimit functor $\Fun ( \cDelta ^{op}, {\mathbf A}) \rightarrow {\mathbf A}$. This is a left Quillen functor if $\Fun ( \cDelta ^{op}, {\mathbf A})$ is endowed with the projective model structure, but not the Reedy model structure. However, the <em>geometric realization</em> functor $X_{\bullet } \mapsto |X_{\bullet } | = \int _{ \cDelta ^{op}}(X_{\bullet }, \Delta ^{\bullet })$ is a left Quillen functor with respect to the Reedy model structure. </p>
